---
export const prerender = true;

import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocCallout from "../../../components/docs/DocCallout.astro";
import DocCode from "../../../components/docs/DocCode.astro";
import DocRelated from "../../../components/docs/DocRelated.astro";

const toc = [
  { title: "A throwaway server", href: "#setup", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Is it alive", href: "#health", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Where am I", href: "#where", depth: 2 },
  { title: "What can it do", href: "#capabilities", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Sessions", href: "#sessions", depth: 2 },
  { title: "Files and search", href: "#files", depth: 2 },
  { title: "The empty shapes", href: "#empty", depth: 2 },
  { title: "The event feed", href: "#events", depth: 2 },
  { title: "What errors look like", href: "#errors", depth: 2 },
  { title: "The whole tour as a script", href: "#script", depth: 2 },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="A guided tour with real responses"
  subtitle="Every request on this page was run against a real nikcli server, and every response is what actually came back."
  description="A hands-on tour of the nikcli HTTP API with real captured responses: health, paths, projects, tools, sessions, files, search, the event feed and error shapes."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="setup" class="doc-section">
    <h2>A throwaway server</h2>
    <p>
      The fastest way to learn this API is to hit it. Point <code>NIKCLI_TEST_HOME</code> somewhere
      disposable and the server keeps its sessions, config and database there instead of in your
      real one — so you can create and delete freely.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`mkdir -p ~/demo && cd ~/demo && git init -q
printf 'export function greet(name: string) {\\n  // TODO: localise this\\n  return \`Hello, \${name}!\`\\n}\\n' > greet.ts
printf '# demo\\n' > README.md
git add -A && git commit -qm initial

# an isolated home, so nothing here touches your real sessions or config
NIKCLI_TEST_HOME=~/demo/.nikcli-home \\
  nikcli serve --hostname 127.0.0.1 --port 4199`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Everything below is copied from a real run">
      <p>
        The responses on this page were captured from exactly that setup. The only edit is
        cosmetic: the long temporary paths were rewritten to <code>/Users/me/demo</code>, and two
        very long arrays are marked where they were trimmed.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="health" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Is it alive</h2>
    <p>
      Health is instance-less — it needs no project and no auth on loopback, which makes it the
      right thing to poll while waiting for a server to come up.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s http://127.0.0.1:4199/global/health`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{"healthy":true,"version":"local","revision":"local"}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>version</code> reads <code>local</code> here because this server was run from a source
      checkout; an installed build reports its release.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="where" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Where am I</h2>
    <p>
      <code>/path</code> answers the question every integration asks first: which directories is
      this instance actually using? Note <code>home</code> pointing at the throwaway — that is the
      isolation working.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/path?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{
  "home": "/Users/me/demo/.nikcli-home",
  "state": "/Users/me/demo/.nikcli-home/state",
  "config": "/Users/me/demo/.nikcli-home/config",
  "worktree": "/Users/me/demo",
  "directory": "/Users/me/demo"
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>/project/current</code> resolves the project for the directory you selected. The
      <code>id</code> is a stable hash of the worktree, so it is the same on every call and across
      restarts.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/project/current?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{
  "id": "c6d9c4f98cc34c8bfb0a40b3a60eca6f624750e6",
  "worktree": "/Users/me/demo",
  "canonical": "/Users/me/demo",
  "vcs": "git",
  "time": { "created": 1789656731762, "updated": 1789656731762 },
  "sandboxes": []
}`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="request + response">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/vcs?directory=$PWD"
{"branch":"main"}`}</DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="capabilities" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What can it do</h2>
    <p>
      The tool registry is the honest answer to "what can the agent actually do here". This is the
      real list from a default install — and the endpoint a plugin author checks to confirm a
      registered tool arrived.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/experimental/tool/ids?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`["invalid","question","bash","monitor","read","tree","glob","grep","edit","write",
 "multiedit","task","delegation","context_collect","context_related","context_diagnostics",
 "memory_search","generate_image","artifact","webfetch","todowrite","todoread","create_goal",
 "get_goal","update_goal","websearch","codesearch","repo_clone","repo_overview","skill",
 "apply_patch","lsp","plan_exit","plan_enter","speak","voice","opentui","advisor","delegator",
 "search_tools","code_mode","browser_control","computer","herdr"]`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>/agent</code> returns each agent with its resolved permission table, which is where
      those tools get gated. Trimmed heavily here — the real payload is long.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/agent?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response (trimmed)">{`[
  {
    "name": "build",
    "mode": "primary",
    "native": true,
    "permission": [
      { "permission": "*",             "pattern": "*",            "action": "allow" },
      { "permission": "browser_control","pattern": "*",           "action": "ask"   },
      { "permission": "computer",      "pattern": "*",            "action": "ask"   },
      { "permission": "read",          "pattern": "*",            "action": "allow" },
      { "permission": "read",          "pattern": "*.env",        "action": "ask"   },
      { "permission": "read",          "pattern": "*.env.example","action": "allow" }
      /* … trimmed … */
    ]
  }
  /* … more agents … */
]`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Permission rules are ordered and last-match-wins">
      <p>
        Notice <code>read: *</code> allowed, then <code>read: *.env</code> set to ask, then
        <code>read: *.env.example</code> allowed again. Reading the list top to bottom is the only
        way to predict what a given path will do.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="sessions" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Sessions</h2>
    <p>A fresh project has none:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/session?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`[]`}</DocCode>
    <p>Creating one returns the whole session, not just an id:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:4199/session?directory=$PWD" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"title":"api tour"}'`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{
  "id": "ses_f50250f8fffexTvasNDaQeiLXP",
  "slug": "swift-eagle",
  "projectID": "c6d9c4f98cc34c8bfb0a40b3a60eca6f624750e6",
  "directory": "/Users/me/demo",
  "title": "api tour",
  "version": "local",
  "time": { "created": 1789656756337, "updated": 1789656756337 },
  "skills": []
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>slug</code> is a human-readable handle generated alongside the id —
      <code>swift-eagle</code> is easier to say out loud than
      <code>ses_f50250f8fffexTvasNDaQeiLXP</code>. <code>projectID</code> matches what
      <code>/project/current</code> returned, which is how a session stays bound to its project.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="files" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Files and search</h2>
    <p>
      File listing is project-relative, and carries <code>ignored</code> so a client can grey out
      what git is not tracking.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/file?path=.&directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`[
  { "name": "greet.ts",  "path": "greet.ts",  "absolute": "/Users/me/demo/greet.ts",  "type": "file", "ignored": false },
  { "name": "README.md", "path": "README.md", "absolute": "/Users/me/demo/README.md", "type": "file", "ignored": false }
]`}</DocCode>
    <p>Reading returns content as a tagged object rather than a bare string:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/file/content?path=greet.ts&directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{
  "type": "text",
  "content": "export function greet(name: string) {\\n  // TODO: localise this\\n  return \`Hello, \${name}!\`\\n}"
}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      Text search returns ripgrep's own match structure — line number, byte offset and the span of
      each match within the line, which is enough to highlight without re-searching client side.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/find?pattern=TODO&directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`[
  {
    "path":  { "text": "greet.ts" },
    "lines": { "text": "  // TODO: localise this" },
    "line_number": 2,
    "absolute_offset": 38,
    "submatches": [{ "match": { "text": "TODO" }, "start": 5, "end": 9 }]
  }
]`}</DocCode>
    <p>Filename search is fuzzy, which is worth knowing before you treat it as a filter:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/find/file?query=greet&directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`["greet.ts","README.md"]`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="Both files matched &quot;greet&quot;">
      <p>
        <code>README.md</code> came back too. <code>/find/file</code> ranks by fuzzy match rather
        than filtering by substring, so treat the order as meaningful and the tail as noise.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="empty" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The empty shapes</h2>
    <p>
      Worth seeing once, because "empty" is not spelled the same way everywhere. Most collections
      return a bare array; loops and missions return an object with the collection inside it.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="text" title="real responses, idle project">{`GET /session/{id}/message   ->  []
GET /session/{id}/todo      ->  []
GET /session/{id}/diff      ->  []
GET /file/status            ->  []
GET /permission             ->  []
GET /question               ->  []
GET /pty                    ->  []
GET /lsp                    ->  []
GET /experimental/workspace ->  []
GET /loop                   ->  {"loops":[],"runtimes":[]}
GET /mission                ->  {"missions":[],"runtimes":[]}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      That asymmetry is not an accident — the loop and mission payloads carry
      <code>runtimes</code> alongside the definitions — but it will catch you if you assume every
      list endpoint returns an array.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="events" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The event feed</h2>
    <p>
      The feed is plain server-sent events. Connecting to an idle instance gets you the handshake
      and then silence until something happens.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -sN "http://127.0.0.1:4199/event?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="text" title="response">{`data: {"type":"server.connected","properties":{}}`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>server.connected</code> arriving is the confirmation your subscription is live — which
      is exactly the moment it is safe to submit a prompt without losing the first tokens. See
      <a href="/docs/guides/streaming">Streaming a response</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="errors" class="doc-section">
    <h2>What errors look like</h2>
    <p>A declared domain error carries its name and data:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s -w '\\nHTTP %{http_code}\\n' "http://127.0.0.1:4199/session/ses_doesnotexist?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{"name":"NotFoundError","data":{"message":"Session not found: ses_doesnotexist"}}
HTTP 404`}</DocCode>
    <p>A body that fails schema validation reports the exact path that was wrong:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s -w '\\nHTTP %{http_code}\\n' \\
  -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:4199/session/ses_x/message?directory=$PWD" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{}'`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{
  "data": {},
  "error": [{
    "expected": "array",
    "code": "invalid_type",
    "path": ["parts"],
    "message": "Invalid input: expected array, received undefined"
  }],
  "success": false
}
HTTP 400`}</DocCode>
    <p>
      That error array is the schema validator's own output, so <code>path</code> points at the
      field — here, a missing <code>parts</code> on a prompt.
    </p>
    <p>Two responses that are not errors, and surprise people:</p>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s -w '\\nHTTP %{http_code}\\n' "http://127.0.0.1:4199/file/content?path=nope.ts&directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="json" title="response">{`{"type":"text","content":""}
HTTP 200`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="A missing file reads as empty, not as 404">
      <p>
        <code>/file/content</code> answers 200 with <code>content: ""</code> for a path that does
        not exist. If your code distinguishes "empty file" from "no file", check with
        <code>/file</code> first — the status code will not tell you.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
    <DocCode language="bash">{`curl -s "http://127.0.0.1:4199/nope?directory=$PWD"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCode language="html" title="response — HTTP 200">{`<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <title>NikCLI</title>
    ...`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="warn" title="An unknown path returns the web app, not a 404">
      <p>
        Unmatched non-<code>/mobile</code> paths fall through to the hosted web app so it can be
        served from the same origin. So a typo in an endpoint gives you an HTML page with status
        200 — if a client suddenly starts failing to parse JSON, check the path before anything
        else.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="script" class="doc-section">
    <h2>The whole tour as a script</h2>
    <p>
      Run this in the demo project with the server up, and you get every response on this page in
      one go.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash" title="tour.sh">{`#!/usr/bin/env bash
# tour.sh — run the whole thing against a throwaway server
set -euo pipefail

BASE="http://127.0.0.1:4199"
DIR="$PWD"

say() { printf '\\n\\033[1m== %s\\033[0m\\n' "$1"; }
get() { curl -s -m 20 "$BASE$1"; echo; }

say "health";        get "/global/health"
say "paths";         get "/path?directory=$DIR"
say "project";       get "/project/current?directory=$DIR"
say "vcs";           get "/vcs?directory=$DIR"
say "tools";         get "/experimental/tool/ids?directory=$DIR"
say "files";         get "/file?path=.&directory=$DIR"
say "grep TODO";     get "/find?pattern=TODO&directory=$DIR"

say "create session"
SESSION=$(curl -s -X POST "$BASE/session?directory=$DIR" \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' -d '{"title":"api tour"}' | jq -r .id)
echo "$SESSION"

say "session";       get "/session/$SESSION?directory=$DIR"
say "messages";      get "/session/$SESSION/message?directory=$DIR"
say "diff";          get "/session/$SESSION/diff?directory=$DIR"

say "errors"
curl -s -w ' <- HTTP %{http_code}\\n' "$BASE/session/ses_nope?directory=$DIR"`}</DocCode>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Then throw it away">
      <p>
        Because the server ran with <code>NIKCLI_TEST_HOME</code> pointed at
        <code>~/demo/.nikcli-home</code>, deleting that directory removes every session and every
        row this tour created. Nothing touched your real state.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <DocRelated
    links={[
      { title: "Using the API", href: "/docs/guides/api", description: "The same endpoints, with the SDK alongside curl." },
      { title: "API Reference", href: "/docs/api", description: "Every endpoint, field by field." },
      { title: "Streaming a response", href: "/docs/guides/streaming", description: "What arrives on the feed once work starts." },
      { title: "Building an interface", href: "/docs/guides/custom-interface", description: "Turning these responses into a client." },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
